A common phrase in recent conversations has been "the third place" or a lack thereof in our city lives. As a result, of which, it has become rather common for peopl\
A recent weekend-afternoon-naptime sesh spent in meandering thoughts took me back to a place. A place introduced to me at childhood. A place that's been a third place for generations in my family without being labelled so.\
Kalna Gate is a humble railway crossing. Not as well known outside the equally humble town of Barddhaman in Bengal. Since my close family –&nbsp;brother, uncle and grand parents have all lived near this unmarked landmark, I've grown many summers nearby.\
It's not a cafe, not even a tea stall where people gather for an adda and yet it used to attract numerous nearby residents, all of whom –&nbsp;after a tiring summer day of work would spend their evenings with each other. My late grand father was one of them. Sitting between two tracks, his group would chat over topics unknown as the passing trains ushered in the dark.\
As a kid fascinated by trains I would go the same crossing, with my father – sit on a fence near one of the gates and just be. Watch trains shake the earth –&nbsp;often losing count of the number of coaches, I'd look for sparks on the overhead pantograph wires specially during twilight in the dark –&nbsp;sometimes chat with my father and other times just feel the breeze.\
I've kept this tradition alive till date. After I got married, I introduced M to this place and the atmosphere around. Fascinatingly even after a year, she recognized it flawlessly from my impressionist painting drawn from memory.\
I'm pretty sure whatever gravity the place posesses, will keep pulling me back there. It's not supposed to be a third palce and yet it is.
